[b]Directional Probability Engine v3[/b] answers: what's the most likely next directional leg from here — a continuation of the current trend, or a reversal of it — and what target can it reach before invalidation. Continuation and Reversal are not two separate detectors; they're two readings of the same retracement ratio against the same reference leg.

[b]The key idea[/b]

Take the most recently confirmed leg (bullish low-to-high, or bearish high-to-low) and measure how deep the current counter-move has retraced against it.

[list]
[*][b]Continuation Maturity[/b] — a bell curve on that retracement centered near a typical pullback depth, adapting to how clean the reference leg was. Bet: the reference leg's own direction resumes.
[*][b]Reversal Maturity[/b] — a second bell curve on the exact same retracement, centered near a full round-trip. Bet: the reference leg is invalidated and a fresh opposite leg is starting.
[/list]

A bullish reference leg feeds Long Continuation AND Short Reversal. A bearish reference leg feeds Short Continuation AND Long Reversal — always mirrored, never a hardcoded direction.

[b]What feeds Readiness[/b]

[list]
[*][b]Reference-leg quality[/b] — efficiency and overlap accumulated bar-by-bar and frozen the instant a pivot confirms, so quality always refers to the same leg the retracement is measured against.
[*][b]Maturity[/b] — the price bell curve above, multiplied by a time-maturity factor (the counter-move's duration relative to the reference leg's own duration).
[*][b]Exhaustion[/b] — DETERIORATION: recent-bars efficiency compared against the counter-move's own running average, not a static "is it choppy" snapshot — plus momentum deceleration and confirmed-pivot divergence.
[/list]

These three combine as a floored-multiplicative gate, not a weighted average — a strong reference leg alone can no longer carry the score when Maturity hasn't actually developed.

[b]What feeds Confirmation[/b]

An explicit state machine (Untriggered → Broken → Confirmed → Accepted), entered by either:

[list]
[*]a same-bar [b]rejection at the extreme[/b] — a marginal new high/low that closes back into the range — the only genuinely leading ingredient, since it fires before any pivot confirms
[*]a micro-swing structure break — faster-confirming than the main structure, but still a confirming sensor, not a leading one
[/list]

Acceptance is read directly from how long price has held in the Confirmed/Accepted state — not a rolling count that allows non-consecutive closes.

[b]Target and Invalidation[/b]

Frozen the instant a Trigger confirms and drawn directly on the price chart:

[list]
[*][b]T1[/b] — the nearest opposite-type confirmed pivot, validated against a minimum distance (falls back to an ATR projection if price already passed it)
[*][b]T2[/b] (optional) — an extension beyond T1, sized by the counter-move's own range
[*][b]Invalidation[/b] — the counter-move's running extreme plus an ATR buffer
[/list]

A reward:risk feasibility factor gates the final Score, same-bar Target/Invalidation conflicts resolve conservatively (invalidation first), and a fresh opposite-direction trigger cancels the other side's still-open setup.

[b]Display[/b]

Color-coded histogram only — no table in the oscillator pane. Light shade = Readiness building, solid shade = confirmed Score. Every per-role score is available in the data window.

[b]Important[/b]

These are rule-based 0–100 evidence scores, not statistically calibrated hit-rate probabilities. A separate outcome/backtest model would be required to translate a score into an empirical hit rate.

